PlatformIO tests for the Gravity FlexSeq project.

This directory holds TWO categories of test that must never be mixed. They live
in two distinct PlatformIO environments, and that is deliberately structural:
it is what lets the acceptance suite stay a reliable signal.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. FlexSeq acceptance / regression  ->  env `native`
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    test_cv_gate, test_main_screen, test_paged_screen, test_pattern,
    test_pattern_bank, test_pattern_screen, test_persistence,
    test_sequencer_engine, test_subdiv, test_transport, test_trigger_sequencer,
    test_ui_controller

Pure C++ domain, no Arduino and no libGravity. MUST be green: a phase is not
finished while one of these fails. 209 assertions as of 2026-08-22.

Each of the four added since 2026-08-22 was verified by MUTATION, not merely by
being green: test_ui_controller 22/22 mutations killed, test_main_screen 20/20,
test_persistence 17/17, and the footer additions to test_paged_screen and
test_pattern_screen 7/8 -- the survivor there being an equivalent mutant, which
ADR 0001 records rather than counts as a pass. Their TypeScript mirrors were
swept the same way (15/15, 15/15, 22/22).

    ./tools/run-cpp-tests.sh [test_name]

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. libGravity characterization  ->  env `native_libgravity`
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    test_analog_input, test_button, test_clock, test_digital_output,
    test_encoder, test_gravity

libGravity headers (pinned at commit 9be88be1f4) compiled against the Arduino
stubs in `test/mocks/`. These tests describe the REAL behaviour of the pinned
dependency, defects included: 7 assertions out of 68 fail by construction.
Never weaken them to obtain green — that would hide a defect of the dependency.

Since 2026-08-20, `test_gravity` additionally CHARACTERIZES `Gravity::Process()`:
it reads exactly two analog inputs — CV1 and CV2, once each — and polls both
buttons plus the encoder switch. FlexSeq no longer calls that function, the
converter now belonging to an ISR (PRD 10.6). So the risk is not missing an
upstream change — the dependency is pinned by decision — but forgetting to
re-audit that function at the next pin bump. These assertions turn that
oversight into a failure. A twin test verifies that the pieces called instead
cover the same inputs with ZERO analog reads: that is the invariant which makes
the workaround correct. The per-pin read counters live in `test/mocks/Arduino.h`.

The criterion for this suite is therefore NOT "everything passes", it is
"conforms to the audit". A module that reproduces exactly the audited anomalies
is GREEN. Red is reserved for drift: a failure outside the reference, or an
audited anomaly that starts passing — which would mean the pinned dependency
changed under our feet.

The three ways of making those 7 assertions green are all closed off: patching
the dependency (forbidden, it is pinned), weakening the tests (forbidden, and
that is what hid the breakage for four months), or working around it on the
FlexSeq side — which is the adapter layer's job, but changes nothing about
libGravity's behaviour, so these tests would stay red, and rightly so.

    ./tools/run-libgravity-tests.sh

The script does more than run the suite: it compares the set of failing
assertions against the audited reference and reports drift in either direction
(an anomaly that disappears, one that appears). An expected red is not a signal;
a red that changes is one.

It produces its own report and MASKS the `pio test` transcript: a per-module
recap, then the list of reproduced anomalies with the libGravity defect each one
documents. `VERBOSE=1` restores the raw transcript, `VERBOSE=2` shows it with
`-vvv`.

Prerequisite: the AVR env must have been built once, the headers being read from
`.pio/libdeps/nanoatmega328/`. The script takes care of it when needed.

Why the pio transcript is masked — three of its outputs mislead on
`platform = native`:

  - it presents the Unity binary's exit code (= the number of failing
    assertions) as a signal: "SIGINT (Interrupt: 2)" for 2 failures, "SIGHUP
    (Hangup: 1)" for 1. Neither is a crash;
  - the resulting ERRORED status is therefore unusable, and it hides the
    distinction between "fails as expected" and "does not compile";
  - its total is wrong: it adds one pseudo-assertion per ERRORED module.

A practical annoyance on top: PlatformIO strips its colours as soon as its
output is captured, which makes PASSED and FAILED indistinguishable in a log.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Both, plus the TypeScript model
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    ./tools/run-all-tests.sh                # all three suites
    ./tools/run-all-tests.sh --cpp          # C++ acceptance only
    ./tools/run-all-tests.sh --ts           # TypeScript only
    ./tools/run-all-tests.sh --libgravity   # characterization only

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
History — why this README exists
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The six characterization tests were introduced by `0574600`, where the `native`
env carried `-I.pio/libdeps/nanoatmega328/libGravity/src -Itest/mocks`. Commit
`f0d6134` (PR #1) rewrote that env for the domain tests: it removed those two
`-I` flags AND added `test_filter = test_pattern*` in the same commit. The tests
therefore stopped compiling without ever being collected — so without emitting
any signal at all, for four months.

The lesson is structural: a `test_filter` that narrows collection can hide a
compilation failure. That is why the two categories now each have their own
environment, and why the characterization suite is run by default by
`run-all-tests.sh`.
